## 2. FIRST-GLANCE TEST
Within 3 seconds a new user understands this is an instructional slide asking them to classify background points across six datasets; the six scatter plots are visually prominent but unlabeled as to what "classify" means or what the axes represent. The three apparent actions are: (1) read each dataset label, (2) inspect each scatter plot, (3) determine a class for background points â none of these are interactive controls, so they are "actions" only in the cognitive sense. Given the injected VIEW purpose (SVM/kernels/QP lecture frame), the focal point should be the datasets themselves, and they do win the eye due to the colorful points against white plot areas, but the instructional intent ("Check classâ¦ background point") is slightly grammatically awkward and competes with the colorful data for attention.

## 3. STRENGTHS
- The six dataset panels are evenly spaced and aligned in a clean grid, giving immediate scannability across all examples.
- Each plot uses distinct, saturated colors for different point clusters, making cluster separation visually obvious.
- The dataset label bars above each plot clearly identify the source file for every panel.

